<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Navic talks to a self-hosted server whose address the user types in at runtime, so this
   config cannot name the hosts it trusts. Two separate permissions were being granted
   globally, and only one of them is actually required.

   cleartextTrafficPermitted stays true. A LAN Navidrome on plain HTTP is a supported
   setup, and Android has no way to express "cleartext for whatever host the user
   configured" in a static file. Deployments reachable from outside the LAN should
   terminate TLS at a reverse proxy — that is a documented requirement for the hub and for
   the Chromecast stream URLs anyway (PROTOCOL §12, CLAUDE.md §3).

   User-installed CAs are now debug-only. They were trusted in the base config, which
   applied to EVERY HTTPS destination the app touches — Navidrome, the hub, AudioMuse,
   lb-bot, cover art — so anyone who could get a certificate installed on the device could
   read all of it. debug-overrides is the Android-sanctioned scope for that: it applies
   only to a debuggable build, which is where intercepting proxies belong.

   NOTE: a release build no longer trusts user CAs, so a self-signed HTTPS server needs its
   certificate in the system store (or the server needs a publicly-trusted certificate).
   Plain HTTP to such a server keeps working unchanged.

   The Chromecast v2 socket is a deliberate, separate exception: it speaks TLS to a
   self-signed device certificate on the LAN and uses its own trust-all socket factory
   inside CastChannel. That factory must never be handed to a general HTTP client.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
	<!--
		cleartextTrafficPermitted is needed for people who are
		using Navidrome instances that are on LAN/use http
	-->
	<base-config
		cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
		tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
		<trust-anchors>
			<certificates src="system" />
		</trust-anchors>
	</base-config>
	<debug-overrides>
		<trust-anchors>
			<certificates src="system" />
			<certificates
				src="user"
				tools:ignore="AcceptsUserCertificates" />
		</trust-anchors>
	</debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
